  "verbalization": "Nortriptyline, a tricyclic antidepressant, is linked to Slc6a2 through a series of mechanistic pathways that involve the modulation of taste perception and the binding of the drug to the Slc6a2 transporter. The evidence suggests that Nortriptyline, along with its metabolites Trimipramine and Doxepin, causes Taste Peculiar, a side effect that is mediated by the Slc6a2 protein. This binding occurs at the level of the Tricyclic Antidepressant class, indicating a shared pharmacological profile. The integration of these pathways highlights a consistent mechanism where the drug's effects are linked to the Slc6a2 transporter, suggesting a potential role in the pathophysiology of taste disturbances. The recurring evidence points to a direct interaction between Nortriptyline and Slc6a2, supported by the presence of a common ancestor in the Tricyclic Antidepressant class, which strengthens the mechanistic link. The observed scores reflect a robust and well-supported relationship, though further experimental validation would be necessary to confirm the exact functional role of Slc6a2 in this context.",
